messages:
mina: quick repro: long-running generator + auth retry = process won't exit after ctx cancel. generator returns but a background refresh goroutine is stuck in a backoff sleep loop that never sees ctx.Done().

zack: can you paste the minimal snippet?

mina: here's the essence: 
```go
func refresher(ctx context.Context) {
  for {
    tok, err := fetchToken() // may retry/backoff internally
    if err != nil {
      time.Sleep(backoff) // not context-aware
      continue
    }
    _ = tok
    select { case <-time.After(time.Minute): default: }
    if ctx.Err() != nil { return }
  }
}
```

mina: we used context cancel for main stream but forgot that the refresh loop's backoff/sleep doesn't respect ctx. process hangs.

leah: onboarding DX hit: sampleapp shows naive refresher pattern. users assume ctx cancels everything.

zack: short fixes: 1) sampleapp: switch to signal.NotifyContext and use context-aware sleep/select for backoffs; 2) SDK: provide NewContextAwareTokenSource wrapper that runs refresh with a ctx and cancels in-flight waits.

leah: also consider generator API ergonomics: return a Stream object with Next(ctx) so callers can pass per-iteration ctx and shutdown is explicit. maybe as v2.

mina: i'll open a tiny PR updating the sample and add a README blurb "Make token refresh cancellable in long-lived streams."

ci-bot: Created ticket RW-8309: "go sdk: context-aware token refresh adapter + sampleapp guidance" and queued CI.

zack: add a unit test that simulates a stalled token endpoint (net/http server that delays) and assert process teardown under N seconds. that'll catch regressions.

mina: thanks all, i'll include the test in the sampleapp PR and link the ticket.
